# Types, Variables, and Control Flow

C# is **statically typed**: every variable has a type fixed at compile time, and the compiler rejects mismatches before your program runs.

## Value types and reference types

```csharp
int count = 42;              // value type
double price = 19.99;
bool isReady = true;
char grade = 'A';
string name = "Kodstigen";   // reference type, the variable holds a reference

var total = count * price;   // var = "infer the type", still strictly double
const int MaxRetries = 3;    // compile-time constant
```

`var` is not `dynamic`. `var total = count * price;` compiles to exactly `double total = ...`, the compiler simply wrote the type for you.

## Strings

```csharp
string first = "Ada";
string last = "Lovelace";

string full = $"{first} {last}";     // interpolation
string padded = $"{first,-10}|";     // "Ada       |"
string money = $"{1234.5:C}";        // culture-aware currency
string path = @"C:\repos\CodeForge"; // verbatim string, backslashes are literal
```

Strings are **immutable**: every concatenation allocates a new one. Building a string in a loop belongs in a `StringBuilder`.

```csharp
var sb = new System.Text.StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) sb.Append(i).Append(',');
Console.WriteLine(sb.ToString());   // 0,1,2,3,4,
```

## Control flow

```csharp
int score = 87;

if (score >= 90) Console.WriteLine("A");
else if (score >= 80) Console.WriteLine("B");
else Console.WriteLine("C or below");

// switch expression: produces a value, no break statements
string label = score switch
{
    >= 90 => "excellent",
    >= 80 => "good",
    >= 60 => "passing",
    _     => "needs work",
};

for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) Console.WriteLine(i);

foreach (var word in new[] { "build", "test", "ship" })
    Console.WriteLine(word);

int n = 5;
while (n > 0) { Console.Write(n + " "); n--; }
```

The `switch` **expression** with `=>` arms and `_` for the default case is the modern form. The old `switch` statement with `case:` and `break;` still exists, but reach for the expression whenever you are producing a value.

## Nullability

```csharp
string? maybeName = null;                 // ? means "this may be null"
string safe = maybeName ?? "anonymous";   // null-coalescing
int? length = maybeName?.Length;          // null-conditional, result is int?
```

With `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>` in the csproj, the compiler warns whenever you dereference something that might be null. Treat those warnings as errors: they are the single biggest source of runtime crashes in real .NET code.

> [!WARNING]
> `int` overflows silently by default: `int.MaxValue + 1` wraps around to `int.MinValue` instead of throwing. Wrap the arithmetic in a `checked { }` block whenever a wrap-around would be a bug rather than a feature.

# Methods, Classes, and Objects

A **class** bundles data (fields, properties) with behavior (methods). An **object** is one instance of that class.

## A class with properties and a constructor

```csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    private decimal _balance;                       // field: internal state

    public string Owner { get; }                    // read-only auto-property
    public decimal Balance => _balance;             // expression-bodied property
    public string Currency { get; set; } = "SEK";   // with a default value

    public BankAccount(string owner, decimal opening = 0m)
    {
        Owner = owner;
        _balance = opening;
    }

    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(amount));
        _balance += amount;
    }

    public bool TryWithdraw(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount > _balance) return false;
        _balance -= amount;
        return true;
    }

    public override string ToString() => $"{Owner}: {_balance:0.00} {Currency}";
}
```

```csharp
var account = new BankAccount("Ada", 500m);
account.Deposit(250m);
Console.WriteLine(account.TryWithdraw(1000m)); // False
Console.WriteLine(account);                    // Ada: 750,00 SEK
```

Use `decimal`, not `double`, for money. `double` is binary floating point and cannot represent `0.1` exactly, which shows up as öre quietly disappearing after a few thousand transactions.

## Method signatures worth knowing

```csharp
public static int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;             // static: no instance needed
public static int Sum(params int[] numbers) => numbers.Sum();
public static bool TryParseAge(string s, out int age)     // out: a second return value
    => int.TryParse(s, out age);

Console.WriteLine(Add(b: 3, a: 2));   // named arguments, order-free
Console.WriteLine(Sum(1, 2, 3, 4));   // 10
```

## Records: data types for free

```csharp
public record Point(int X, int Y);

var p1 = new Point(1, 2);
var p2 = p1 with { Y = 9 };                // non-destructive copy
Console.WriteLine(p1 == new Point(1, 2));  // True, value equality
Console.WriteLine(p2);                     // Point { X = 1, Y = 9 }
```

A `record` gives you a constructor, value-based `Equals` and `GetHashCode`, a readable `ToString`, and `with` expressions from a single line. Use records for data you pass around, classes for things with identity and mutable state.

## Static members and object initializers

```csharp
public class Counter
{
    public static int Created { get; private set; }
    public string Label { get; init; } = "";
    public Counter() => Created++;
}

var c = new Counter { Label = "requests" };   // object initializer
Console.WriteLine(Counter.Created);           // 1, lives on the type, not the instance
```

`init` makes a property settable in the object initializer and read-only afterwards, immutability without writing a constructor for every combination of fields.

> [!NOTE]
> `class` is a reference type: assigning it copies the reference, so two variables refer to the same object. `struct` (and `record struct`) is a value type: assigning it copies the whole value. Default to `class` or `record` unless you are writing a small, immutable, performance-sensitive value.

# OOP in .NET: Inheritance, Interfaces, Polymorphism

## Inheritance and virtual members

```csharp
public abstract class Employee
{
    public required string Name { get; init; }
    public abstract decimal MonthlyPay();          // no body: subclasses must supply one
    public virtual string Describe() => $"{Name} earns {MonthlyPay():0} per month";
}

public class Salaried : Employee
{
    public decimal AnnualSalary { get; init; }
    public override decimal MonthlyPay() => AnnualSalary / 12;
}

public class Hourly : Employee
{
    public decimal Rate { get; init; }
    public int Hours { get; init; }
    public override decimal MonthlyPay() => Rate * Hours;
    public override string Describe() => base.Describe() + $" ({Hours} h)";
}
```

- `abstract` members have no implementation and force every subclass to provide one.
- `virtual` members have a default that a subclass *may* replace with `override`.
- `base.Describe()` calls up to the parent version.
- `required` makes the compiler reject `new Salaried()` unless `Name` is set; `init` makes the property settable only during initialization.

## Polymorphism in action

```csharp
Employee[] staff =
{
    new Salaried { Name = "Ada", AnnualSalary = 720_000m },
    new Hourly   { Name = "Linus", Rate = 450m, Hours = 120 },
};

foreach (var e in staff)
    Console.WriteLine(e.Describe());   // each object runs its own override
```

One loop, one static type (`Employee`), two behaviors selected at runtime. That is polymorphism.

## Interfaces: contracts without inheritance

```csharp
public interface IPayable
{
    decimal MonthlyPay();
    string Currency => "SEK";     // default implementation, optional to override
}

public class Contractor : IPayable
{
    public decimal Invoiced { get; init; }
    public decimal MonthlyPay() => Invoiced;
}
```

A class inherits from **one** base class but can implement **many** interfaces. That is why the BCL is built on interfaces: `IEnumerable<T>`, `IComparable<T>`, `IDisposable`, `IEquatable<T>`.

```csharp
public class Batch : IDisposable
{
    public void Dispose() => Console.WriteLine("cleaned up");
}

using (var b = new Batch()) { /* work */ }   // Dispose called on the way out
using var b2 = new Batch();                  // same thing, scoped to the enclosing block
```

## Pattern matching over a hierarchy

```csharp
static string Tag(Employee e) => e switch
{
    Salaried { AnnualSalary: > 1_000_000m } => "senior salaried",
    Salaried => "salaried",
    Hourly { Hours: > 160 } h => $"overworked ({h.Hours} h)",
    _ => "other",
};
```

Property patterns (`{ Hours: > 160 }`) let you branch on shape and value at once, and the compiler warns when your arms are unreachable or your switch is not exhaustive.

> [!WARNING]
> Prefer interfaces and composition over deep inheritance chains. Three or four levels of `: BaseThing` is the point where changing a base method starts silently breaking subclasses you had forgotten existed.

# Collections, Generics, and a First Taste of LINQ

## The collections you will actually use

```csharp
var list = new List<string> { "build", "test" };
list.Add("ship");
list.Insert(0, "plan");
Console.WriteLine(list.Count);   // 4
Console.WriteLine(list[2]);      // test

var byId = new Dictionary<int, string> { [1] = "Ada", [2] = "Linus" };
byId[3] = "Grace";
if (byId.TryGetValue(2, out var who)) Console.WriteLine(who);   // Linus

var unique = new HashSet<int> { 1, 2, 2, 3 };  // Count == 3
var queue = new Queue<string>();               // FIFO: Enqueue / Dequeue
var stack = new Stack<string>();               // LIFO: Push / Pop
```

| Need | Type | Lookup cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ordered, index access | `List<T>` | O(1) by index, O(n) by value |
| Key to value | `Dictionary<K,V>` | O(1) average |
| Membership only | `HashSet<T>` | O(1) average |
| Fixed size, fastest | `T[]` | O(1) by index |

Use `TryGetValue` instead of `ContainsKey` followed by `[key]`: it hashes once instead of twice.

## Generics: one implementation, many types

```csharp
public class Box<T>
{
    private readonly List<T> _items = new();
    public void Add(T item) => _items.Add(item);
    public T this[int i] => _items[i];
    public int Count => _items.Count;
}

var names = new Box<string>();
names.Add("Ada");
// names.Add(42);   // compile error: T is string here

public static T Largest<T>(IEnumerable<T> items) where T : IComparable<T>
{
    T best = items.First();
    foreach (var item in items)
        if (item.CompareTo(best) > 0) best = item;
    return best;
}
```

The `where T : IComparable<T>` **constraint** is what allows the method body to call `CompareTo` at all. Without a constraint, `T` could be anything, so the compiler only lets you treat it as `object`.

## LINQ, the short version

```csharp
var people = new List<(string Name, int Age)>
{
    ("Ada", 36), ("Linus", 54), ("Grace", 45), ("Margaret", 33),
};

var names = people
    .Where(p => p.Age > 35)
    .OrderBy(p => p.Name)
    .Select(p => p.Name)
    .ToList();                          // ["Ada", "Grace", "Linus"]

Console.WriteLine(people.Count(p => p.Age < 40));    // 2
Console.WriteLine(people.Average(p => p.Age));       // 42
Console.WriteLine(people.Any(p => p.Name == "Ada")); // True
```

`Where`, `Select`, `OrderBy`, `Count`, `Sum`, `Any`, `First`: these extension methods work on *any* `IEnumerable<T>`, whether that is an array, a list, the lines of a file, or a database table. The Intermediate course takes this much further.

> [!NOTE]
> `List<T>.Add` is amortised O(1), but when the internal array fills up it allocates a new one at double the size and copies everything across. If you know roughly how many items you will add, `new List<T>(capacity: 1000)` skips every one of those copies.

# Exceptions, Files, and NuGet Packages

## Exceptions

```csharp
try
{
    var text = File.ReadAllText("config.json");
    var port = int.Parse(text);
}
catch (FileNotFoundException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"No config at {ex.FileName}, using defaults.");
}
catch (FormatException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("config.json did not contain a number.");
    throw;                       // rethrow, preserving the original stack trace
}
finally
{
    Console.WriteLine("done");   // runs whether or not it threw
}
```

Rules that save real debugging time:

- Catch the **specific** exception. A bare `catch (Exception)` that swallows everything turns a crash into a mystery.
- `throw;` rethrows and keeps the stack trace. `throw ex;` resets it and destroys the evidence.
- Do not use exceptions for expected outcomes. `int.TryParse` returning `false` beats catching `FormatException` in a loop, because throwing is expensive.

```csharp
public class InsufficientFundsException : Exception
{
    public InsufficientFundsException(decimal missing)
        : base($"Missing {missing:0.00} to complete the transfer") { }
}
```

## Reading and writing files

```csharp
var path = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "notes.txt");

File.WriteAllText(path, "first line\n");
File.AppendAllLines(path, new[] { "second line" });

foreach (var line in File.ReadLines(path))   // streams, does not load the whole file
    Console.WriteLine(line);

Console.WriteLine(File.Exists(path));
Directory.CreateDirectory(Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "out"));
```

Always build paths with `Path.Combine` rather than gluing strings together with `/` or `\`. That is the difference between code that works on Linux and code that only works on your laptop.

## JSON

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

record Settings(string Env, int Port);

var json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new Settings("dev", 5000));
// {"Env":"dev","Port":5000}

var back = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Settings>(json)!;
Console.WriteLine(back.Port);   // 5000
```

`System.Text.Json` ships in the BCL, so there is no package to install.

## NuGet: the package ecosystem

```bash
dotnet add package Spectre.Console       # newest stable version
dotnet add package Serilog --version 4.0.0
dotnet list package                      # what this project references
dotnet remove package Serilog
```

The reference lands in your `.csproj`:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <PackageReference Include="Spectre.Console" Version="0.49.1" />
</ItemGroup>
```

Because the csproj is the single source of truth, a fresh clone plus `dotnet restore` reproduces your exact dependency set. There is nothing else to commit and no `node_modules`-sized folder to ignore.

> [!WARNING]
> `File.ReadAllLines` loads the entire file into an array in memory; `File.ReadLines` yields one line at a time. On a multi-gigabyte log file the first one takes your process down and the second one does not notice.
